Context: this is part of the 'variations sur l'équation des lacets', i.e. Grothendieck's study of how Gal(Q̄/Q), or a Grothendieck–Teichmüller-type group, acts on the profinite modular group. The setting is SL(2,Ẑ) with an order-3 element ρ and an order-4/order-2 structure (relation (45): gρ(g)ρ²(g)=ε). An automorphism is described by a 'multiplier' μ∈Ẑ*, the cyclotomic character, together with conjugating matrices. These are ξ=(A B; C D), tied to the order-3 data, and η=(R S; T U), tied to the order-4 data. Entries of ξ are parametrised by quadratic expressions in (a,b,c,d), essentially Eisenstein norm forms; for instance D=δ(c²+d²+cd) with δ=ad−bc=±1. Entries of η are parametrised by expressions in (r,s,t,u), essentially Gaussian norm forms; for instance U=t²+u². This is natural because centralisers of the elliptic elements of SL2 are unit groups of (completed) Z[j] and Z[i].

On the previous page, compatibility (57) between ξ (twisted by 2ν, with μ=2ν+1) and εη forced S=T, hence μD=1 (58), μU=ε (59) and D=εU (60). Here Grothendieck makes (57) explicit as four relations (61): D=εU, μD=1, C=εT, A=ε(R−(μ−1)T). He notes that A and B are then consequences of the others, using C+D−B=1 and AD−BC=1. He rewrites these in the 9 variables a,b,c,d,r,s,t,u,μ as (62):
- μ(c²+d²+cd)=δ;
- c²+d²+cd=εδ(t²+u²);
- an equation for C;
- an equation for A.

A crossed-out paragraph counts dimensions: with (48),(49) one gets 6 equations in 9 variables, so the solution scheme over Spec Z should have relative dimension 3, with relative dimension 1 over each of the 'fibres' (a,b,c,d) and (r,s,t,u). It then considers its Ẑ-points. This is corrected in the margin: 'non, il est de dim. rel 4', i.e. the equations are not independent.

The page ends with a theorem. The multiplier μ is determined by ξ∈SL(2,Ẑ) via μD=1. It is determined up to sign by η via μU=ε, with ε∈{±1} the sign in (45),(56). The next page completes this: ε=ε₂(μ) is fixed by μ mod 4, and δ=ε₃(μ) by μ mod 3. Together the two signs encode μ mod 12, matching (Z/12)*≅{±1}×{±1}. This reflects the abelianisation Z/12 of SL2(Z).
